{"name":"Manifestation / law of attraction for looks","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Manifestation_contre_l%27extr%C3%AAme-droite%2C_Lyon_%2816_juin_2024%29_04.jpg/960px-Manifestation_contre_l%27extr%C3%AAme-droite%2C_Lyon_%2816_juin_2024%29_04.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manifestation_contre_l%27extr%C3%AAme-droite,_Lyon_(16_juin_2024)_04.jpg","caption":"Manifestation contre l'extrême-droite, Lyon (16 juin 2024) 04","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":4,"casual":{"lead":["Manifestation applies the law of attraction to looks: believers visualise the face, body or skin they want and feel it as already real, expecting reality to follow. The payoff people are after is a changed appearance and more confidence in the meantime.","Practitioners combine visualisation, affirmations and an emotional sense of already having the result, and proponents attribute the effect to energy alignment and share personal accounts of success. No published studies test manifestation for physical appearance, and the underlying idea is described as lacking empirical scientific support."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People set aside regular time to visualise their goal look, repeat affirmations and hold the feeling of having achieved it. Some pair it with ordinary routines like skincare, training or grooming."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No direct physical risk is documented. Critics warn it can imply blame for misfortune and undervalue effort, and one study linked belief in it to greater financial risk-taking."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Manifestation_contre_l%27extr%C3%AAme-droite%2C_Lyon_%2816_juin_2024%29_04.jpg/960px-Manifestation_contre_l%27extr%C3%AAme-droite%2C_Lyon_%2816_juin_2024%29_04.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.or